[PDML_Extensions_Demo

    This is a valid PDML document showing examples of PDML extensions.
    For more information about PDML extensions, refer to the
    "PDML Extensions User Manual", available here:
    https://pdml-lang.dev/docs/extensions/user-manual/index.html

    [Comments

        ^// line comment
        Some text ^// trailing line comment

        ^/*
            Block
            comment
            (aka multi-line comment)
        */

        This is ^/* good */ great.

        ^/* nested
            ^/* block
                ^/* comment */
            */
        */

        ^/** A comment containing "*/" **/
    ]

    Note: In the following examples, code prefixed with '→' shows the code
    you would have to write in Core PDML (without using extensions).

    [Unicode_Escape_Sequences

        [Smiley \u{1F600}]
        → [Smiley 😀]

        [Some_math_symbols \u{221A 221E 222B}]
        → [Some_math_symbols √∞∫]
    ]

    ["String Literals"

        [quoted_string_literal

            [square_brackets ^"[[[]]]"]
            → [square_brackets \[\[\[\]\]\]]

            ["tag with spaces" text]
            → [tag\swith\sspaces text]
        ]

        [raw-string-literal

            [regex ^~[\r\n]*\\~]
            → [regex \[\\r\\n\]*\\\\]

            [filePath ^~C:\path\to\my file.txt~]
            → [filePath C:\\path\\to\\my\sfile.txt]
        ]

        [multiLineStringLiteral

            [html_code ^"""
                <div>
                    <p>[Hello]</p>
                </div>
                """
            ]
            → [html_code <div>
    <p>\[Hello\]</p>
</div>]

            ["""
                Tag with 3 lines
                Line 2
                Line 3
                """ ^"""
                Text with 3 lines
                Line 2
                Line 3
                """
            ]
            → [Tag\swith\s3\slines\nLine\s2\nLine\s3 Text with 3 lines
Line 2
Line 3]
        ]
    ]

    ["Command Nodes"

        Notes:
            - You can think of command nodes as function calls that are executed when the document is parsed.
            - Some command nodes are commented out to avoid parser errors due to unavailable resources.

        You can use the 'ins-file' command node to insert the content of a text file.
        Data example:
        ^// ^[ins-file server-config.pdml]
        Markup example:
        ^// ^[ins-file chapter_1.pml]
        ^// ^[ins-file chapter_2.pml]

        Insert text retrieved from a URL:
        ^// ^[ins-url https://example.com/foo/bar.txt]

        Insert the value of OS environment variable 'TEXT_COLOR' (or default to 'white'):
        ^// Text color: ^[ins-env TEXT_COLOR [default white]]

        Insert the value entered by the user in a GUI dialog:
        ^// Text color: ^[ins-gui-input [prompt Enter a color:]]

        Execute an OS command and insert the text written to STDOUT:
        ^// Output of prog.exe: ^[ins-cmdo [cmd path/to/prog.exe --arg1 value_1 --arg2 "value 2"]]

        You can define a constant and then insert it multiple times:
        ^[const [pi 3.1415]]
        Circumference: ^[ins-const pi] * r * 2
        → Circumference: 3.1415 * r * 2
        Area: ^[ins-const pi] * r * r
        → Area: 3.1415 * r * r

        A constant can be used to define the value of a subsequent constant:
        ^[const [website_url https://example.com]]
        ^[const [blog_url ^[ins-const website_url]/blog]]
        Our Blog URL: ^[ins-const blog_url]
        → Our Blog URL: https://example.com/blog

        You can use a command node to define the value of a constant:
        ^/*
            ^[const [pi ^[ins-file ../math/pi.txt]]]
            ^[const [e ^[ins-env E]]]
            ^[const [color ^[ins-gui-input [prompt Enter a color:]]]]
        */
    ]

    [Attributes

        [p ^(id=my-id style="color: green;") This text is green.]

        [rectangle ^(
            color = "light green"
            width = 200
            height = 100)
        ]
        Same rectangle definition using only Core PDML:
        [rectangle
            [color light green]
            [width 200]
            [height 100]
        ]
    ]

    [Java_Scripting_Nodes

        "Java scripting command nodes" allow you to insert Java (not JavaScrip) source code into a PDML document.
        The source code is executed at parse-time and allows you to use the full power of Java to simplify and
        automate the creation of PDML documents.
        Note: This is an experimental feature.

        [ins-exp-demo
            You can use an 'ins-exp' command node to insert a Java expression into a PDML document:
            1 + 1 = ^[ins-exp "1 + 1"]
            → 1 + 1 = 2
            Current date: ^[ins-exp java.time.LocalDate.now()]
        ]

        [script-demo
            You can use a 'script' command node to execute a set of Java statements.

            The following script inserts the Unicode code points U+1F600 to U+1F605 () into the PDML document:
            ^[script
                """
                StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
                for ( int u = 0x1F600; u <= 0x1F605; u++ ) {
                    sb.appendCodePoint ( u );
                }
                ctx.doc().insertText ( sb.toString() );
                """
            ]
            → 😀😁😂😃😄😅
        ]

        [class-demo

            You can define a Java class and then use the class later in the PDML document:

            ^[def
                """
                package test;

                import java.util.List;

                public class MarkupUtils {

                    public static String simpleList ( String... elements ) {

                        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
                        sb.append ( "[list " );
                        for ( String element : elements ) {
                            sb.append ( "[el " )
                              .append ( element )
                              .append ( "]" );
                        }
                        sb.append ( "]" );
                        return sb.toString();
                    }

                    public static String redOnYellow ( String text ) {
                        return "[p ^(html_style=\"color:red; background-color:yellow;\") " + text + "]";
                    }
                }
                """
            ]

            Use the above class to insert a list of fruits:
            [p List of fruits: ^[ins-exp """
                test.MarkupUtils.simpleList (
                    "apple",
                    "orange",
                    "banana" )
                """]]
            → List of fruits: [list [el apple][el orange][el banana]]

            Use the above class to insert a "red on yellow" text:
            ^[ins-exp ~test.MarkupUtils.redOnYellow ( "Warning!" )~]
            → [p ^(html_style="color:red; background-color:yellow;") Warning!]
        ]
    ]

    ["User Defined Command Nodes"

        Besides using standard PDML command nodes, you can define your own command nodes,
        and then use them later in the document (experimental feature).

        Create a new command node:
        ^[script
            """
            import dev.ps.pdml.cmdnode.*;

            CommandNode commandNode = new CommandNodeBuilder ( "my_cmd_node" )
                .executor ( (args, context, tag) ->
                    new CommandNodeResult ( "Simple example of a user-defined command node.", false ) )
                .build();

            ctx.addCommandNode ( commandNode );
            """
        ]

        Use the new command node:
        ^[my_cmd_node]
        → Simple example of a user-defined command node.

        You can store user defined command nodes in external files and then import them into different
        PDML documents using 'ins-file' command nodes.
    ]
]
